#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115024 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66534e01-fdc6-522f-b90a-59a8118bc67b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Complex Root of a Quadratic Equation Given the First Root"}] :question/id #uuid "9cb68d4c-a7a7-5bb8-8905-4bb1ee1dc7e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5776" :question/problem "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root $x=5+4\\textrm{i}.$ What is the other root?" :question/worked-solution "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that $x=5+4\\textrm{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate $x=5-4\\textrm{i}$ is also a root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115025 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "4a2ea20e-6982-541a-90c2-7cb4547a357e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5777" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-3\\textrm{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of $x^2$ in the equation is $2,$ find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-3\\textrm{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x = 3\\textrm{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3\\textrm{i})(x+3\\textrm{i})= 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm 3\\textrm{i},$ but the coefficient of $x^2$ is not $2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}+9) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+18 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115026 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "a6f4c4fe-c7e6-5cde-a2ee-ac25feb97f76" :question/math-academy-id "e-5778" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=6-8\\textrm{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of $x^2$ in the equation is $-1,$ find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=6-8\\textrm{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=6+8\\textrm{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Bigl(x-(6-8\\textrm{i})\\Bigr)\\Bigl(x-(6+8\\textrm{i})\\Bigr) = 0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(6+8\\text{i})-x(6-8\\text{i})+(6-8\\text{i})(6+8\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x-8x\\text{i}-6x+8x\\text{i}+36-64{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x+36+64 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x+100 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x= 6 \\pm 8\\textrm{i},$ but the coefficient of $x^2$ is not $-1.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-12x+100) & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+12x-100 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277913 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1762f5c-a828-593a-b476-2a0506e1ffc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277915} {:answer/id #uuid "e36f6615-42cf-51c3-843c-23cbb69c46cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277916} {:answer/id #uuid "0fae7175-8034-5d13-a670-ce76e142d890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277917} {:answer/id #uuid "b10a96ed-38ea-55c1-b195-dbbe9450bea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277918} {:answer/id #uuid "094dee23-d7d6-5224-9d44-4d2d32346b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1762f5c-a828-593a-b476-2a0506e1ffc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277915} :answer-field/id #uuid "f08fc1fe-886f-55ff-9c40-ae82cb5085de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c0e4e3f6-996e-5dd9-bca4-ac8109f31dab" :question/math-academy-id "q-160005" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $4$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:  \n\n$$\n(x-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})(x+\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\n   \n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $4.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d01e41f0-e212-520d-95f0-74bec61d7121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-13x+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277922} {:answer/id #uuid "a7162c1f-8403-56e9-b166-77c202871017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-8x+16=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277923} {:answer/id #uuid "864dc1dc-f3e9-5499-abeb-685bebbf5189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-4x+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277924} {:answer/id #uuid "610adbdd-1dce-505b-b0be-e08186378084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+4x+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277925} {:answer/id #uuid "1c4d435e-940d-518b-89df-90a42c0fc5b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+16x+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "864dc1dc-f3e9-5499-abeb-685bebbf5189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-4x+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277924} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9cef55a-b4d1-5a31-a5ef-b5d62868f746" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e9fca3c-0e3f-5f42-89f3-8866c444b9c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-16935" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $4$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))(x-(\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})-x(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})+(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})(\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+x\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-\\frac{x}{2}-x\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}+\\frac{1}{4}-3{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{13}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $4.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}-x+\\frac{13}{4}) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-4x+13 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d502d8e-3a85-5fe0-a6a4-683601c2b3e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+75x+18=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277929} {:answer/id #uuid "625dfea3-ebe3-58b0-bcab-4f1f4b0a331f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+6x+25=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277930} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ab96b3-f892-5b70-9a9d-e24a064d4a17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+25x+6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277931} {:answer/id #uuid "43eb36c1-1817-502c-b215-7ae69013e2c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}-18x-25=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277932} {:answer/id #uuid "b140914f-1374-5e37-a3da-d3426f753b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+18x+75=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b140914f-1374-5e37-a3da-d3426f753b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+18x+75=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277933} :answer-field/id #uuid "18ac9bc7-2bf3-5f8c-b9d4-5fcb9fdd6e9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e559008a-7b37-5496-9372-56da3e8e43d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-16936" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-3+4\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $3$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-3+4\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-3-4\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-3+4\\text{i}))(x-(-3-4\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-3-4\\text{i})-x(-3+4\\text{i})+(-3+4\\text{i})(-3-4\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x+4x\\text{i}+3x-4x\\text{i}+9-16{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9+16 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=-3\\pm 4\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $3.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}^{2}+6x+25) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+18x+75 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4140de87-6a61-54ce-9302-f2d5b140842a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{3}x+8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277936} {:answer/id #uuid "da3e800b-b782-5093-9946-7e5612d11fa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x+16=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277937} {:answer/id #uuid "c1991f7c-f9be-50b5-bcd9-86c62d03659a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x+8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277938} {:answer/id #uuid "a143baa9-8ac6-5da9-a60d-9352e0d26b25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-8\\sqrt{3}x+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277939} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb7d1bc-39a4-5a15-9e3f-2ec6e81c29f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-6\\sqrt{3}x+18=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1991f7c-f9be-50b5-bcd9-86c62d03659a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x+8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277938} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c581f7-ce31-5634-a76f-a47e922c2ff9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "def05a71-1ca9-54ef-8d91-ee996f9ae29c" :question/math-academy-id "q-16938" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $2$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}))(x-(\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i})-x(\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i})+(\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i})(\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x\\sqrt{3}-x\\text{i}-x\\sqrt{3}+x\\text{i}+3+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{3}x+3+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{3}x+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\sqrt{3}\\pm \\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{3}x+4) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x+8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66534e01-fdc6-522f-b90a-59a8118bc67b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Complex Root of a Quadratic Equation Given the First Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2c2b632-72f7-5989-b57c-4b1678401a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+10i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2c2b632-72f7-5989-b57c-4b1678401a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+10i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277943} :answer-field/id #uuid "5960a12d-950f-574e-a3b6-3c0ab284b417" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cec409b3-2182-5120-91c1-0546b456be50" :question/math-academy-id "q-274979" :question/problem "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root ${x}_{1}=-2-10\\text{i}.$ What is the other root? **Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.**\n\n${x}_{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that ${x}_{1}=-2-10\\text{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate ${x}_{2}=-2+10\\text{i}$ is also a root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66534e01-fdc6-522f-b90a-59a8118bc67b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Complex Root of a Quadratic Equation Given the First Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff16e412-3fba-53e2-8653-08f6832192cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2p+3qi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277946} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea1f456-322f-531f-98b9-85e064c298ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2p+3qi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff16e412-3fba-53e2-8653-08f6832192cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2p+3qi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277946} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d67deee-4b2c-5bcb-b5e5-0d6035e650f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "63189c11-c337-562b-a544-c32670443f6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-281297" :question/problem "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root ${x}_{1}=2p-3q\\text{i}.$ What is the other root? **Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.**\n\n${x}_{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that ${x}_{1}=2p-3q\\text{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate ${x}_{2}=2p+3q\\text{i}$ is also a root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66534e01-fdc6-522f-b90a-59a8118bc67b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Complex Root of a Quadratic Equation Given the First Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c66e3a8-908a-536d-88ee-fc9eceedc7ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+2ri" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c66e3a8-908a-536d-88ee-fc9eceedc7ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+2ri" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277950} :answer-field/id #uuid "46889a6b-0d3d-560d-a9e5-12768451803a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4e8927ac-77d5-5915-a4b6-94ff662f769d" :question/math-academy-id "q-281300" :question/problem "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root ${x}_{1}=4-2r\\text{i}.$ What is the other root? **Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.**\n\n${x}_{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that ${x}_{1}=4-2r\\text{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate ${x}_{2}=4+2r\\text{i}$ is also a root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e24ca91-8ef8-5b5f-8b1a-afb8aafc4d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25x^2+49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277953} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3546cc-de96-500d-abf3-c5549084d191" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25x^2-49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e24ca91-8ef8-5b5f-8b1a-afb8aafc4d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25x^2+49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277953} :answer-field/id #uuid "02afbf49-4d5a-5d46-9d05-62a18fc8c607" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30b9a300-fc91-58f5-b861-39886df00af7" :question/math-academy-id "q-281325" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $25,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5})(x+\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{7\\text{i}}{5})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{25}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{49}{25} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{7\\text{i}}{5},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $25.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $25.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25({x}^{2}+\\frac{49}{25}) & =0 \\\\ 25{x}^{2}+49 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3a630b7-39d0-53be-9c9c-73924db46c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25x^2+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3a630b7-39d0-53be-9c9c-73924db46c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25x^2+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277957} :answer-field/id #uuid "88981c5f-8f0f-5e76-875f-020ddf19d5f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9844b4bd-f63c-55f4-b3ff-74927a03bd17" :question/math-academy-id "q-281332" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $25,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5})(x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{25}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{25} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}}{5},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $25.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $25.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{25}) & =0 \\\\ 25{x}^{2}+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115588 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b65ba28c-241a-52bb-8865-ac4f88d29192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + x - 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112929} {:answer/id #uuid "261cf6a9-75c6-576e-aa16-b962a0de2823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} - 2x = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112930} {:answer/id #uuid "41be664d-71b0-5226-8974-fc5ae7037fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} - x + 1 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112931} {:answer/id #uuid "45e2a6ac-ac5c-5b04-b2e1-b97bbdd3a86c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + 2x + 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112932} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab86ab2-6d83-5cff-93d2-51aa95e549e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45e2a6ac-ac5c-5b04-b2e1-b97bbdd3a86c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + 2x + 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112932} :answer-field/id #uuid "230cf8eb-448c-56e4-a4c8-a98d9a2b5fd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b3e3d2a-46f3-5327-8b01-7846488f9aea" :question/math-academy-id "q-28215" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-1+\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-1+\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-1-\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-1+\\text{i}))(x-(-1-\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-1+\\text{i})-x(-1-\\text{i})+(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-x\\text{i}+x+x\\text{i}+1-{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "637c2717-30b4-5e15-8382-7ef96fabbeac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}-30x-65=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277960} {:answer/id #uuid "c466de8b-39cd-5efb-a312-656e8d6abc92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}+6x+65=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277961} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e3282f-e6ea-592a-ad47-e4db2d129ebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}+30x+65=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277962} {:answer/id #uuid "2597656f-b4b6-578f-b9eb-d72012b86274" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}+65x+30=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277963} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d63285-206c-5943-b15b-851bfbf50262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}+30x+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4e3282f-e6ea-592a-ad47-e4db2d129ebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}+30x+65=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277962} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e694c51-d109-5447-8152-52cb1df0ecd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "184cd0a3-85ba-5b1e-a01b-94f6bbbf00ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-28224" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-3-2\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $5$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-3-2\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-3+2\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-3-2\\text{i}))(x-(-3+2\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-3+2\\text{i})-x(-3-2\\text{i})+(-3-2\\text{i})(-3+2\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x-2x\\text{i}+3x+2x\\text{i}+9-4{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+13 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=-3\\pm 2\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}+6x+13) & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+30x+65 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66534e01-fdc6-522f-b90a-59a8118bc67b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Complex Root of a Quadratic Equation Given the First Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd805d20-efbe-5d3f-8f4c-a700bd004c26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113055} {:answer/id #uuid "d95eb18e-4241-5d70-8f9b-19a98387ca20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3 + \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113056} {:answer/id #uuid "5a8c5e4b-c6ce-5fe2-a1bb-b0443679074b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - 3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113057} {:answer/id #uuid "6398d19f-2bb5-581b-85f3-7e6a812b7c32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 + \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113058} {:answer/id #uuid "75ccfac7-80fc-5471-bb71-558ad2e5cf83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 + 3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6398d19f-2bb5-581b-85f3-7e6a812b7c32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 + \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113058} :answer-field/id #uuid "12d5025f-6e72-5e2d-ad0f-108caae8ca89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c748dd1-062a-5866-ad04-0894e90b8522" :question/math-academy-id "q-28502" :question/problem "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root $x=3-\\text{i}.$ What is the other root?" :question/worked-solution "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that $x=3-\\text{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate $x=3+\\text{i}$ is also a root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66534e01-fdc6-522f-b90a-59a8118bc67b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Complex Root of a Quadratic Equation Given the First Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6d3b103-7d25-52eb-9a0e-beefcbea3acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2-7\\text{i}}{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277967} {:answer/id #uuid "94300848-1ce9-5bbb-87df-95618adccc5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-7+2\\text{i}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277968} {:answer/id #uuid "2b02e1c3-458a-5178-a9d4-66cbb7678460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2-7\\text{i}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277969} {:answer/id #uuid "7ffeae2d-b2bc-5835-8218-6c5e20b03696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-7-2\\text{i}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277970} {:answer/id #uuid "70f20245-0354-5966-8cb3-f8092935ad48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-7\\text{i}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b02e1c3-458a-5178-a9d4-66cbb7678460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2-7\\text{i}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277969} :answer-field/id #uuid "e67a9389-4500-550e-83fa-3e9dfdf1e1db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "214567ea-5ef3-53f3-8260-3c9198efee57" :question/math-academy-id "q-28519" :question/problem "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{-2+7\\text{i}}{2}.$ What is the other root?" :question/worked-solution "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that $x=\\frac{-2+7\\text{i}}{2}=-1+\\frac{7}{2}\\text{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate $x=-1-\\frac{7}{2}\\text{i}=\\frac{-2-7\\text{i}}{2}$ is also a root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af31cb05-1759-5b66-80d6-9f1c252014ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^{2} - 12 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113073} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9fe814-709a-5981-a8cd-e1d6ef1ce0ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^{2} - 4 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113074} {:answer/id #uuid "f83b13d2-c942-5f6d-848c-b96b9f52e280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^{2} + 6 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113075} {:answer/id #uuid "37840ade-d6f7-53f9-bcd4-90d1ebf43172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^{2} + 4 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113076} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4474bf-1c3a-5e2a-90fe-83794808b92d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^{2} + 12 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e4474bf-1c3a-5e2a-90fe-83794808b92d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^{2} + 12 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113077} :answer-field/id #uuid "8793497f-a347-567e-87f5-112ad45df6d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6f01fd2-19c8-5c6d-b91d-8da801444f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-28523" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=2\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $3$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=2\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=-2\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm 2\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $3.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "faf4fa59-c7e0-5104-be05-c85f60e7b421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277974} {:answer/id #uuid "e46f587e-164c-530c-8904-2d3915a941a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277975} {:answer/id #uuid "91a4b701-2061-5820-b257-7fdf43ff4fa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277976} {:answer/id #uuid "cadb80af-5fa6-5ec2-bdc0-5b948f1970bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}+6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277977} {:answer/id #uuid "e2595612-a299-58d2-9c64-ecba8751d7d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e46f587e-164c-530c-8904-2d3915a941a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277975} :answer-field/id #uuid "c702422a-718f-5f93-b4a0-dde2e4d353da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bcd2e909-ecd9-5092-bf02-27cd4d48ec9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-83584" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $6$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2})(x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{4}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $6.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $6.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
